From 904836d5281374625c33d59e2df8f9f721f60a4c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 08:58:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Corridor grid: corridor relative to the river for sloping
 rivers (R 10)

R 10 H builds the corridor of the curvilinear corridor grid from the bed
less than H above the river instead of the bed below one level (R 3 wl).
With one level, a sloping river (Lenne: 20 m drop over 8.4 km) gets
either a corridor that is only the channel upstream or one that takes
the whole valley floor downstream.

- thalweg: least-cost path over the bed (cost exp((z - zmin)/2 m) per
  length) between the lowest cells of the two wet edge runs (below R 2)
  on the inflow and outflow sides; with R 10 this path, smoothed over
  R 8, is also the centreline (with R 7 1 the given line stays the
  centreline and the thalweg runs between the lowest cells near its
  ends)
- river bed level: the bed along the thalweg smoothed over 200 m of its
  length (pools, saddles, bridges), at every raster cell the level of
  its nearest thalweg cell
- corridor: bed below that level + H, then as before (main component,
  holes filled, dry margin R 3 m, side inlets R 5, cuts at the ends)

Without R 10 the corridor grid is unchanged (Lenne setup with R 3 and
the 26 curvilinear and multi-block SFLOW/NHFLOW regression cases of
REEF3D: grids and results bitwise identical).

Lenne, R 3 130 5 -> R 10 10, R 8 50, R 4 0 140: 3.47 -> 2.49 km2,
249k -> 164k columns, widths in the middle reach 220-310 m -> 65-190 m,
the 400 m3/s flood area (F 65 2) still inside, no folded cells, max
non-orthogonality 49 deg.

